-- check/bad: `+`, `-`, `*` and `//` on two `Int32`s, which is the mistake
-- every reader of `core/Int32.beni` makes once.
--
-- Three `kind_mismatch` and, for `//`, two `type_mismatch`: `+`, `-` and
-- `*` are `number`-kinded so the kind is what fails, while `//` is
-- `Basics.idiv : Int, Int -> Int` and fails as an ordinary mismatch, once
-- per operand. `Int32` is deliberately neither (`fast-compiler.md` §3.1):
-- an operator that read like `Int`'s would hide the one thing the type
-- exists to say, which is that its arithmetic WRAPS. The hint is the whole
-- point of the fixture: the generic "`+`, `-`, `*` and `/` work on numbers
-- only. To join text use `++`." sends an `Int32` reader looking for a
-- conversion that does not exist, so a mismatch with `Int32` on either side
-- gets its own sentence naming `Int32.add` and friends
-- (`src/check/Diagnostics.zig`, `kindNotSatisfied`).
--
-- `Int32NoOperatorsMixed` is the other half: `Int` and `Int32` in the same
-- call, which is an ordinary `type_mismatch` naming both.
import Int32 exposing (Int32)


sum : Int32, Int32 → Int32
sum a b = a + b


difference : Int32, Int32 → Int32
difference a b = a - b


product : Int32, Int32 → Int32
product a b = a * b


quotient : Int32, Int32 → Int32
quotient a b = a // b
